module tecs.internal.framepacket

--[[
Extraction for the Rust-owned GPU lane.

One host-endian packet crosses the managed ABI per completed frame. Nupp owns
world queries, camera selection, depth, draw ordering, material numbering, and
the render graph's declaration; Rust validates the packet and owns targets,
pipelines, culling, compaction, submission, and presentation. Image pixels
never ride in a packet. They cross once through the residency commands
`tecs.gfx.images` queues, so a frame carries only the id a batch binds.

# Packet version 4

Every field is little or big endian to match the host, every integer is
unsigned, and every real is a 32-bit float. Offsets are in bytes from the start
of the packet.

## Header, 128 bytes

| Offset | Type  | Field             | Contract                                             |
| ------ | ----- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| 0      | `u32` | `magic`           | `0x54454353`, and nothing else is a Tecs packet.      |
| 4      | `u32` | `version`         | `4`. A reader rejects every other value.              |
| 8      | `u32` | `headerSize`      | `128`. The graph section starts after it.             |
| 12     | `u32` | `flags`           | Bit 0 enables the shadow lane, bit 1 bloom.           |
| 16     | `u32` | `graphRevision`   | A reader rebuilds its graph when this changes.        |
| 20     | `u32` | `graphBytes`      | Byte length of the graph section.                     |
| 24     | `u32` | `batchStride`     | `20`.                                                 |
| 28     | `u32` | `batchCount`      | Zero only when `instanceCount` is zero.               |
| 32     | `u32` | `instanceStride`  | `80`.                                                 |
| 36     | `u32` | `instanceCount`   | How many instances follow the batch table.            |
| 40     | `f32` | `targetWidth`     | Render target width in pixels, greater than zero.     |
| 44     | `f32` | `targetHeight`    | Render target height in pixels, greater than zero.    |
| 48     | `f32` | `cameraX`         | View center x in world units.                         |
| 52     | `f32` | `cameraY`         | View center y in world units, increasing downward.    |
| 56     | `f32` | `cameraZoom`      | Greater than zero.                                    |
| 60     | `f32` | `cameraRotation`  | Radians, turning the scene counter-clockwise.         |
| 64     | `u32` | `lightStride`     | `32`.                                                 |
| 68     | `u32` | `lightCount`      | At most 256, the ceiling one frame resolves.          |
| 72     | `u32` | `casterCount`     | Instances carrying a cast flag, which sizes the list. |
| 76     | `u32` | reserved          | Zero.                                                 |
| 80     | `f32` | `ambientR`        | Ambient red, at or above zero.                        |
| 84     | `f32` | `ambientG`        | Ambient green.                                        |
| 88     | `f32` | `ambientB`        | Ambient blue.                                         |
| 92     | `f32` | `shadowSteps`     | March samples at full attenuation, four or more.      |
| 96     | `f32` | `shadowHeight`    | World height a caster at height one stands.           |
| 100    | `f32` | `shadowMargin`    | World units outside the view a caster is kept.        |
| 104    | `f32` | `dropOpacity`     | Drop-shadow darkness at full weight, zero to one.     |
| 108    | `f32` | `dropLength`      | Longest a drop shadow may be, in world units.         |
| 112    | `f32` | `bloomThreshold`  | Brightness a pixel passes to be extracted.            |
| 116    | `f32` | `bloomKnee`       | Width of the threshold's soft knee, above zero.       |
| 120    | `f32` | `bloomIntensity`  | Scales the blurred contribution.                      |
| 124    | `u32` | reserved          | Zero.                                                 |

The shadow and bloom fields are written whether or not their flag is set, so a
backend reads one layout and the flag alone decides. A reader validates them
only when the flag they belong to is set.

## Graph section, `graphBytes` bytes at offset 128

The pass and target declaration, as `tecs.gpu.passes.section` encodes it. It
rides in every packet rather than being sent once, so a backend that lost its
device, its surface, or its whole graphics object rebuilds from the next frame
instead of holding state the sender cannot see. `graphRevision` is what keeps
that cheap: a reader parses the section only when the revision differs from the
one it built its graph from.

## Light table, `lightCount` entries of 32 bytes after the graph section

| Offset | Type  | Field       | Contract                                        |
| ------ | ----- | ----------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| 0      | `f32` | `x`         | World x of the light.                           |
| 4      | `f32` | `y`         | World y, increasing downward.                   |
| 8      | `f32` | `height`    | Height above the surface plane in world units.  |
| 12     | `f32` | `radius`    | Reach in world units, greater than zero.        |
| 16     | `f32` | `r`         | Red.                                            |
| 20     | `f32` | `g`         | Green.                                          |
| 24     | `f32` | `b`         | Blue.                                           |
| 28     | `f32` | `intensity` | Scales the contribution, at or above zero.      |

Lights are ordered by ascending entity id, so the same scene bins and casts the
same way every frame however the archetypes are laid out. A world holding more
than the ceiling keeps the lowest ids and drops the rest.

## Batch table, `batchCount` entries of 20 bytes after the light table

| Offset | Type  | Field           | Contract                                                |
| ------ | ----- | --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| 0      | `u32` | `image`         | A `tecs.gfx.images` id, or `0` for the white fallback.   |
| 4      | `u32` | `sampler`       | Below `tecs.gfx.images.SAMPLER_COUNT`.                   |
| 8      | `u32` | `lane`          | `0` opaque, `1` blended.                                 |
| 12     | `u32` | `firstInstance` | Zero-based index of the batch's first instance.          |
| 16     | `u32` | `instanceCount` | At least one.                                            |

Batches partition the instances exactly: the first starts at zero, each one
starts where the previous ended, and their counts sum to `instanceCount`. They
are runs in draw order rather than a grouping, because the scene is drawn back
to front and reordering by image would reorder the picture.

A run breaks on the lane as well as on the image and the sampler, so every
batch belongs to one lane. That is what lets a backend cull and compact the
instances and still draw a batch: compaction preserves packet order, so the
survivors of a single-lane batch are a contiguous run of that lane's visible
list and one indirect draw covers them.

## Instances, `instanceCount` entries of 80 bytes after the batch table

| Offset | Type  | Field      | Contract                                                     |
| ------ | ----- | ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| 0      | `f32` | `x`        | Center x in world units.                                      |
| 4      | `f32` | `y`        | Center y in world units.                                      |
| 8      | `f32` | `rotation` | Radians, turning the quad clockwise on screen.                |
| 12     | `f32` | `depth`    | Zero nearest to one farthest, from `tecs.gfx.layers.depthOf`. |
| 16     | `f32` | `scaleX`   | Full width in world units, not a half extent.                 |
| 20     | `f32` | `scaleY`   | Full height in world units.                                   |
| 24     | `f32` | `param`    | The material's own scalar, zero to one.                       |
| 28     | `f32` | `castHeight` | Caster height, zero to one of the shadow height. Zero otherwise. |
| 32     | `f32` | `u0`       | Left edge of the sampled region.                              |
| 36     | `f32` | `v0`       | Top edge of the sampled region.                               |
| 40     | `f32` | `u1`       | Right edge of the sampled region.                             |
| 44     | `f32` | `v1`       | Bottom edge of the sampled region.                            |
| 48     | `f32` | `r`        | Red, multiplied into the sampled color.                       |
| 52     | `f32` | `g`        | Green.                                                        |
| 56     | `f32` | `b`        | Blue.                                                         |
| 60     | `f32` | `a`        | Alpha, multiplied into the sampled alpha.                     |
| 64     | `u32` | `material` | A `tecs.gpu.materials` id, below the build's material count.  |
| 68     | `u32` | `flags`    | Bit 0 marks the blended lane and equals the batch's; bit 1 an occluder; bit 2 a drop-shadow caster. |
| 72     | `u32` | reserved   | Zero.                                                         |
| 76     | `u32` | reserved   | Zero.                                                         |

Bits 1 and 2 are exclusive of each other and neither is ever set with bit 0. An
entity carrying both `Occluder2D` and `DropShadow2D` is an occluder, because
dropping that half would silently unblock a light, and a blended entity casts
nothing at all: it never reaches the G-buffer, so a hard silhouette of it would
be a lie.

The four sixteen-byte groups are what a backend binds as a storage buffer, so
the layout is a `vec4` quadruple followed by four words rather than the field
order a reader would pick.

Instances run in draw order: farthest depth first, and entity id ascending
between equal depths. A backend draws them in that order and the later one
covers the earlier, which is what makes the order deterministic between runs
without a tie-breaker of its own.

Every real in the packet is finite. A backend rejects a packet whose length
does not match its declared counts, whose strides differ from the constants
above, whose batches do not partition its instances, whose instance lane
disagrees with its batch's, or whose material id its dispatch does not carry.
]]

local archetype = require("tecs.internal.archetype")
local images = require("tecs.gfx.images")
local layers = require("tecs.gfx.layers")
local lighting = require("tecs.gfx.lighting")
local materials = require("tecs.gpu.materials")
local passes = require("tecs.gpu.passes")
local query = require("tecs.internal.query")
local rendercomponents = require("tecs.internal.rendercomponents")
local world = require("tecs.internal.world")

--- Identifies a Tecs frame packet, and is a compatibility surface.
export const MAGIC: uint32 = 0x54454353

--- The packet layout this module writes.
export const VERSION: uint32 = 4

--- The byte length of the packet header.
export const HEADER_SIZE: uint32 = 128

--- The byte length of one batch table entry.
export const BATCH_STRIDE: uint32 = 20

--- The byte length of one instance.
export const INSTANCE_STRIDE: uint32 = 80

--- The byte length of one light table entry.
export const LIGHT_STRIDE: uint32 = 32

--- Names the lane that rasterizes into the G-buffer.
---
--- The lane numbering is a compatibility surface shared with the backend's
--- compaction, which fills one lane per dispatch.
export const LANE_OPAQUE: integer = 0

--- Names the lane the forward pass blends over the composited image.
export const LANE_BLEND: integer = 1

--- Marks an instance as belonging to the blended lane.
export const FLAG_BLENDED: integer = 1

--- Marks an instance as an occluder, whose silhouette enters the light mask.
export const FLAG_OCCLUDER: integer = 2

--- Marks an instance as a drop-shadow caster.
export const FLAG_DROP_SHADOW: integer = 4

--- Set in the header when the frame draws the shadow lane.
export const FRAME_SHADOWS: integer = 1

--- Set in the header when the frame draws the bloom chain.
export const FRAME_BLOOM: integer = 2

const narrow = nupp.math.f32.narrow
const wrap = nupp.math.u32.wrap
const huge = math.huge

local function finite(value: number): boolean
    return value == value and value ~= huge and value ~= -huge
end

--- A world-bound reusable renderer query.
---
--- The row arrays grow to a scene's high-water mark and are then reused, so a
--- steady scene extracts without allocating anything but the packet itself.
export record Extractor
    private world: world.World
    private poses: {rendercomponents.Transform2D}
    private lightPoses: {rendercomponents.Transform2D}
    private drawables: query.Query
    private activeCameras: query.Query
    private cameras: query.Query
    private lights: query.Query
    private order: {integer}
    private entities: {integer}
    private depths: {number}
    private transforms: {rendercomponents.Transform2D}
    private tints: {rendercomponents.Tint}
    private imageIds: {integer}
    private samplers: {integer}
    private lanes: {integer}
    private castFlags: {integer}
    private castHeights: {number}
    private materialIds: {integer}
    private params: {number}
    private u0: {number}
    private v0: {number}
    private u1: {number}
    private v1: {number}
    private rowCount: integer
    private sorted: integer
    private before: function(left: integer, right: integer): boolean
    private lightOrder: {integer}
    private lightEntities: {integer}
    private lightTransforms: {rendercomponents.Transform2D}
    private lightValues: {lighting.PointLight2D}
    private lightCount: integer
    private lightsSorted: integer
    private beforeLight: function(left: integer, right: integer): boolean

    packet: function(exclusive self: Extractor, width: integer, height: integer): string
end

--- Creates an extractor and registers its queries with one world.
--- @param value the world whose renderable entities and cameras it reads
--- @return the extractor, which the caller keeps for the world's lifetime
export function new(exclusive value: world.World): Extractor
    local entities: {integer} = {}
    local depths: {number} = {}
    local before = function(left: integer, right: integer): boolean
        const leftDepth = depths[left]
        const rightDepth = depths[right]
        if leftDepth ~= rightDepth then
            -- Farthest first, so the nearer instance is drawn over it.
            return leftDepth > rightDepth
        end

        return entities[left] < entities[right]
    end
    local lightEntities: {integer} = {}
    -- Ascending entity id, so which lights survive the ceiling and which order
    -- a tile accumulates them in are the same for the same scene every frame.
    local beforeLight = function(left: integer, right: integer): boolean
        return lightEntities[left] < lightEntities[right]
    end

    return new Extractor(
        world = value,
        poses = {},
        lightPoses = {},
        drawables = value:newQuery({
            include = {
                rendercomponents.Transform2DComponent,
                rendercomponents.TintComponent,
                rendercomponents.Renderable2D,
            },
        }),
        activeCameras = value:newQuery({
            include = {rendercomponents.Camera2DComponent, rendercomponents.ActiveCamera},
        }),
        cameras = value:newQuery({include = {rendercomponents.Camera2DComponent}}),
        lights = value:newQuery({include = {rendercomponents.Transform2DComponent, lighting.PointLight2DComponent},}),
        order = {},
        entities = entities,
        depths = depths,
        transforms = {},
        tints = {},
        imageIds = {},
        samplers = {},
        lanes = {},
        castFlags = {},
        castHeights = {},
        materialIds = {},
        params = {},
        u0 = {},
        v0 = {},
        u1 = {},
        v1 = {},
        rowCount = 0,
        sorted = 0,
        before = before,
        lightOrder = {},
        lightEntities = lightEntities,
        lightTransforms = {},
        lightValues = {},
        lightCount = 0,
        lightsSorted = 0,
        beforeLight = beforeLight
    )
end

--- Returns the lowest live entity carrying a camera the query matched.
local function pick(value: query.Query): rendercomponents.Camera2D?
    local chosen: rendercomponents.Camera2D? = nil
    local chosenEntity = 0
    for candidate, count in value:iter() do
        local active = candidate as archetype.Archetype
        local cameras: {rendercomponents.Camera2D} = assert(active:get(rendercomponents.Camera2DComponent))
        for index = 1, count as integer do
            const entity = active.entities[index]
            if chosen == nil or entity < chosenEntity then
                chosen = cameras[index]
                chosenEntity = entity
            end
        end
    end

    return chosen
end

--- Returns the view this frame projects through, as center, zoom, and rotation.
---
--- A world with no camera entity draws through a camera centered on the render
--- target at zoom one, which is the mapping that puts one world unit on one
--- target pixel with the origin at the top left corner.
--- @raises when the selected camera's zoom is not greater than zero, or when
---     any of its four values is not finite
local function view(borrows self: Extractor, width: number, height: number): number, number, number, number
    local camera = pick(self.activeCameras)
    if camera == nil then
        camera = pick(self.cameras)
    end
    if camera == nil then
        return width * 0.5, height * 0.5, 1.0, 0.0
    end
    if not (camera.zoom > 0.0) or not finite(camera.zoom) then
        error("framepacket: the active camera's zoom must be greater than zero", 3)
    end
    if not finite(camera.x) or not finite(camera.y) or not finite(camera.rotation) then
        error("framepacket: the active camera holds a value that is not finite", 3)
    end

    return camera.x, camera.y, camera.zoom, camera.rotation
end

--- Reuses a detached pose for interpolation, leaving the simulation value untouched.
local function presented(
    current: rendercomponents.Transform2D,
    previous: rendercomponents.PreviousTransform2D?,
    alpha: number,
    poses: {rendercomponents.Transform2D},
    row: integer
): rendercomponents.Transform2D
    if previous == nil then
        return current
    end
    local pose = poses[row]
    if pose == nil then
        pose = rendercomponents.Transform2DComponent.defaultFactory()
        poses[row] = pose
    end
    pose.x = previous.x + (current.x - previous.x) * alpha
    pose.y = previous.y + (current.y - previous.y) * alpha
    const angle = (current.rotation - previous.rotation + math.pi) % (2 * math.pi) - math.pi
    pose.rotation = previous.rotation + angle * alpha
    pose.z = current.z
    pose.layer = current.layer
    pose.scaleX = current.scaleX
    pose.scaleY = current.scaleY

    return pose
end

--- Reads every renderable row into the extractor's reused arrays.
--- @raises when an entity selects a material id this build does not carry
local function collect(exclusive self: Extractor): nil
    const entities = self.entities
    const depths = self.depths
    const transforms = self.transforms
    const tints = self.tints
    const imageIds = self.imageIds
    const samplers = self.samplers
    const lanes = self.lanes
    const castFlags = self.castFlags
    const castHeights = self.castHeights
    const materialIds = self.materialIds
    const params = self.params
    const u0 = self.u0
    const v0 = self.v0
    const u1 = self.u1
    const v1 = self.v1
    const materialCount = materials.count()
    local _, _, alpha = self.world:getFixedTiming()
    local rows: integer = 0
    for candidate, count in self.drawables:iter() do
        local active = candidate as archetype.Archetype
        local rowTransforms: {rendercomponents.Transform2D} = assert(active:get(rendercomponents.Transform2DComponent))
        const previous = active:get(rendercomponents.PreviousTransform2DComponent)
        local rowTints: {rendercomponents.Tint} = assert(active:get(rendercomponents.TintComponent))
        local rowSprites = active:get(rendercomponents.SpriteComponent) as {rendercomponents.Sprite}?
        local rowMaterials = active:get(materials.MaterialComponent) as {materials.Material}?
        local rowOccluders = active:get(lighting.Occluder2DComponent) as {lighting.Occluder2D}?
        local rowDropShadows = active:get(lighting.DropShadow2DComponent) as {lighting.DropShadow2D}?
        for index = 1, count as integer do
            rows = rows + 1
            const transform = presented(
                rowTransforms[index],
                previous ~= nil and previous[index] or nil,
                alpha,
                self.poses,
                rows
            )
            const tint = rowTints[index]
            entities[rows] = active.entities[index]
            transforms[rows] = transform
            tints[rows] = tint
            depths[rows] = layers.depthOf(transform.layer, transform.z, transform.x, transform.y)
            -- The lane is the tint's alpha and nothing else. The G-buffer pass
            -- writes with replace and has nowhere to put partial coverage, so
            -- an entity that is not fully opaque leaves for the forward pass,
            -- where the depth it already sorted by decides it against the
            -- opaque geometry in front of it.
            const blended = tint.a < 1.0
            lanes[rows] = blended and LANE_BLEND or LANE_OPAQUE
            -- A blended entity casts nothing: it never reaches the G-buffer, so
            -- a hard silhouette of it would be a lie. An entity carrying both
            -- cast components is an occluder, because dropping that half would
            -- silently unblock a light.
            local flag: integer = 0
            local height: number = 0.0
            if not blended then
                if rowOccluders ~= nil then
                    flag = FLAG_OCCLUDER
                    height = rowOccluders[index].height
                elseif rowDropShadows ~= nil then
                    flag = FLAG_DROP_SHADOW
                    height = rowDropShadows[index].height
                end
            end
            castFlags[rows] = flag
            castHeights[rows] = flag == 0 and 0.0 or height
            if rowMaterials == nil then
                materialIds[rows] = 0
                params[rows] = 0.25
            else
                const material = rowMaterials[index]
                if material.id < 0 or material.id >= materialCount then
                    error("framepacket: material id " .. tostring(material.id) .. " is not one this build carries", 3)
                end
                materialIds[rows] = material.id
                params[rows] = material.param
            end
            if rowSprites == nil then
                imageIds[rows] = 0
                samplers[rows] = 0
                u0[rows] = 0.0
                v0[rows] = 0.0
                u1[rows] = 1.0
                v1[rows] = 1.0
            else
                const sprite = rowSprites[index]
                const image = sprite.image
                -- An image that never became resident draws against the
                -- backend's white fallback rather than dropping the entity.
                if image ~= 0 and images.stateOf(image) == "resident" then
                    imageIds[rows] = image
                    samplers[rows] = images.samplerOf(image)
                else
                    imageIds[rows] = 0
                    samplers[rows] = 0
                end
                u0[rows] = sprite.u0
                v0[rows] = sprite.v0
                u1[rows] = sprite.u1
                v1[rows] = sprite.v1
            end
        end
    end
    self.rowCount = rows
end

--- Reads every light entity into the extractor's reused arrays.
--- @raises when a light holds a radius that is not greater than zero, or any
---     value that is not finite
local function collectLights(exclusive self: Extractor): nil
    const lightEntities = self.lightEntities
    const lightTransforms = self.lightTransforms
    const lightValues = self.lightValues
    local _, _, alpha = self.world:getFixedTiming()
    local found: integer = 0
    for candidate, count in self.lights:iter() do
        local active = candidate as archetype.Archetype
        local rowTransforms: {rendercomponents.Transform2D} = assert(active:get(rendercomponents.Transform2DComponent))
        const previous = active:get(rendercomponents.PreviousTransform2DComponent)
        local rowLights: {lighting.PointLight2D} = assert(active:get(lighting.PointLight2DComponent))
        for index = 1, count as integer do
            const light = rowLights[index]
            if not (light.radius > 0.0) or not finite(light.radius) then
                error("framepacket: a PointLight2D radius must be greater than zero", 3)
            end
            if not finite(
                light.height
            ) or not finite(light.r) or not finite(light.g) or not finite(light.b) or not finite(light.intensity) then
                error("framepacket: a PointLight2D holds a value that is not finite", 3)
            end
            found = found + 1
            lightEntities[found] = active.entities[index]
            lightTransforms[
                found
            ] = presented(
                rowTransforms[index],
                previous ~= nil and previous[index] or nil,
                alpha,
                self.lightPoses,
                found
            )
            lightValues[found] = light
        end
    end
    self.lightCount = found
end

--- Orders the collected lights by ascending entity id and applies the ceiling.
local function sortLights(exclusive self: Extractor): nil
    const order = self.lightOrder
    const found = self.lightCount
    for index = 1, found do
        order[index] = index
    end
    for index = found + 1, self.lightsSorted do
        order[index] = nil
    end
    self.lightsSorted = found
    table.sort(order, self.beforeLight)
    if found > lighting.MAX_LIGHTS then
        self.lightCount = lighting.MAX_LIGHTS
    end
end

--- Orders the collected rows farthest first, breaking ties by entity id.
local function sortRows(exclusive self: Extractor): nil
    const order = self.order
    const rows = self.rowCount
    for index = 1, rows do
        order[index] = index
    end
    for index = rows + 1, self.sorted do
        order[index] = nil
    end
    self.sorted = rows
    table.sort(order, self.before)
end

--- Encodes one complete scene packet.
--- @param self the extractor to run
--- @param width the positive render target width in pixels
--- @param height the positive render target height in pixels
--- @return a host-endian versioned packet owned by the caller
--- @raises when a viewport dimension is not positive, when the active camera
---     holds a zoom that is not greater than zero, or when an entity selects a
---     material this build does not carry
function Extractor.packet(exclusive self: Extractor, width: integer, height: integer): string
    if width < 1 or height < 1 then
        error("framepacket: viewport dimensions must be positive", 2)
    end
    const cameraX, cameraY, zoom, rotation = view(self, width, height)
    collect(self)
    sortRows(self)
    collectLights(self)
    sortLights(self)

    const order = self.order
    const imageIds = self.imageIds
    const samplers = self.samplers
    const lanes = self.lanes
    const castFlags = self.castFlags
    const rows = self.rowCount

    -- The batch table is written before the instances, so the runs are counted
    -- once here and the values are read again below rather than staged.
    local batches: integer = 0
    local runImage: integer = -1
    local runSampler: integer = -1
    local runLane: integer = -1
    for index = 1, rows do
        const row = order[index]
        if imageIds[row] ~= runImage or samplers[row] ~= runSampler or lanes[row] ~= runLane then
            batches = batches + 1
            runImage = imageIds[row]
            runSampler = samplers[row]
            runLane = lanes[row]
        end
    end

    local casters: integer = 0
    for index = 1, rows do
        if castFlags[index] ~= 0 then
            casters = casters + 1
        end
    end

    const shadowTuning = lighting.shadows()
    const bloomTuning = lighting.bloom()
    -- A frame with no caster and no light runs the shadow lane over an empty
    -- list, so the flag is the tuning alone and the backend does not have to
    -- learn a second reason a pass might be skipped.
    local frameFlags: integer = 0
    if shadowTuning ~= nil then
        frameFlags = frameFlags + FRAME_SHADOWS
    end
    if bloomTuning ~= nil then
        frameFlags = frameFlags + FRAME_BLOOM
    end
    const ambientR, ambientG, ambientB = lighting.ambient()
    const lightCount = self.lightCount

    const graph = passes.section()
    local buffer = nupp.io.newBuffer()
    local writer = nupp.io.newScalarWriter(buffer)
    writer:writeUint32(MAGIC)
        :writeUint32(VERSION)
        :writeUint32(HEADER_SIZE)
        :writeUint32(wrap(frameFlags))
        :writeUint32(wrap(passes.revision()))
        :writeUint32(wrap(#graph))
        :writeUint32(BATCH_STRIDE)
        :writeUint32(wrap(batches))
        :writeUint32(INSTANCE_STRIDE)
        :writeUint32(wrap(rows))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(width))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(height))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(cameraX))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(cameraY))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(zoom))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(rotation))
        :writeUint32(LIGHT_STRIDE)
        :writeUint32(wrap(lightCount))
        :writeUint32(wrap(casters))
        :writeUint32(0)
        :writeFloat32(narrow(ambientR))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(ambientG))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(ambientB))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(shadowTuning ~= nil and shadowTuning.steps or 24.0))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(shadowTuning ~= nil and shadowTuning.height or 64.0))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(shadowTuning ~= nil and shadowTuning.margin or 200.0))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(shadowTuning ~= nil and shadowTuning.dropOpacity or 0.4))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(shadowTuning ~= nil and shadowTuning.dropLength or 512.0))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(bloomTuning ~= nil and bloomTuning.threshold or 0.8))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(bloomTuning ~= nil and bloomTuning.knee or 0.1))
        :writeFloat32(narrow(bloomTuning ~= nil and bloomTuning.intensity or 0.7))
        :writeUint32(0)
    writer:writeBytes(graph)

    const lightOrder = self.lightOrder
    const lightTransforms = self.lightTransforms
    const lightValues = self.lightValues
    for index = 1, lightCount do
        const slot = lightOrder[index]
        const transform = lightTransforms[slot]
        const light = lightValues[slot]
        writer:writeFloat32(narrow(transform.x))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(transform.y))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(light.height))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(light.radius))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(light.r))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(light.g))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(light.b))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(light.intensity))
    end

    runImage = -1
    runSampler = -1
    runLane = -1
    local runFirst: integer = 0
    local runLength: integer = 0
    for index = 1, rows do
        const row = order[index]
        if imageIds[row] ~= runImage or samplers[row] ~= runSampler or lanes[row] ~= runLane then
            if runLength > 0 then
                writer:writeUint32(wrap(runImage))
                    :writeUint32(wrap(runSampler))
                    :writeUint32(wrap(runLane))
                    :writeUint32(wrap(runFirst))
                    :writeUint32(wrap(runLength))
            end
            runImage = imageIds[row]
            runSampler = samplers[row]
            runLane = lanes[row]
            runFirst = index - 1
            runLength = 0
        end
        runLength = runLength + 1
    end
    if runLength > 0 then
        writer:writeUint32(wrap(runImage))
            :writeUint32(wrap(runSampler))
            :writeUint32(wrap(runLane))
            :writeUint32(wrap(runFirst))
            :writeUint32(wrap(runLength))
    end

    const depths = self.depths
    const transforms = self.transforms
    const tints = self.tints
    const castHeights = self.castHeights
    const materialIds = self.materialIds
    const params = self.params
    const u0 = self.u0
    const v0 = self.v0
    const u1 = self.u1
    const v1 = self.v1
    for index = 1, rows do
        const row = order[index]
        const transform = transforms[row]
        const tint = tints[row]
        writer:writeFloat32(narrow(transform.x))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(transform.y))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(transform.rotation))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(depths[row]))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(transform.scaleX))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(transform.scaleY))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(params[row]))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(castHeights[row]))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(u0[row]))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(v0[row]))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(u1[row]))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(v1[row]))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(tint.r))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(tint.g))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(tint.b))
            :writeFloat32(narrow(tint.a))
            :writeUint32(wrap(materialIds[row]))
            :writeUint32(wrap((lanes[row] == LANE_BLEND and FLAG_BLENDED or 0) + castFlags[row]))
            :writeUint32(0)
            :writeUint32(0)
    end
    writer:drop()
    local packed = buffer:getString()
    buffer:drop()

    return packed
end
